Overall verdict

This set has major authenticity problems. A18_CRPA_Post_Payment_Audit_Finding_Letter.pdf is plausible in concept but contains drafting defects and questionable audit demands. A20_CAQH_Provider_Data_Portal_History.pdf is the stronger tell because it appears to combine CAQH terminology with a fictional or substantially altered portal model.

Highest-confidence concerns

CAQH and “ProviderLink” are conflated

The filename and data-source field identify the CAQH Provider Data Portal, while the document brands the product as “ProviderLink Directory Exchange.” The report treats ProviderLink as the portal, audit-log service, directory exchange, profile repository, attestation engine, and nightly payer feed. Someone familiar with CAQH workflows would likely question whether this is an authentic CAQH export.

The portal history contains an impossible future state

The export was generated on 04/10/2026, but the NY registration entry says the registration was updated on 08/14/2024 from an expiration of 08/12/2024 to 08/12/2026. The source event itself is internally possible. However, the event describes the two-year physician registration pattern already identified as incorrect throughout the document set.

The attestation history does not follow its asserted 120-day cycle

Full attestations are listed on 11/02/2023, 02/28/2024, 06/27/2024, 08/26/2024, and 02/27/2025. The August 2024 to February 2025 interval visibly exceeds the document’s stated 120-day cycle, even though the February event says the profile was “attested at 120-day cycle.” The wording conflicts with the dates shown.

The audit asks NPPES to support an EIN

A18_CRPA_Post_Payment_Audit_Finding_Letter.pdf requests confirmation of the organizational entity’s “federal Employer Identification Number as recorded with NPPES.” This is questionable wording. The letter should identify the actual authoritative source or enrollment evidence expected for the tax identifier rather than implying that an ordinary NPPES confirmation supplies the EIN in the same way it supplies the NPI.

The payer claims it cannot identify its own authoritative billing record

CRPA says it compared its enrollment and claims data against Coopa Blue’s current billing organization, found a mismatch, but cannot determine which identifier is authoritative. That is a plausible reason to request clarification, but the letter does not state either competing NPI, the claims affected, payer enrollment record, billing TIN, service dates, or contractual basis for the supposed correct relationship. It identifies a mismatch without giving enough information to reproduce it.

A18_CRPA_Post_Payment_Audit_Finding_Letter.pdf
The opening sentence is grammatically broken. “The audit was conducted pursuant to CRPA’s standard post-payment review procedures directory maintenance, and enrollment representations” is missing connecting language.
The requested-response list is malformed. Item 1 ends with “applicable to the rendering provider identified above for” and continues nowhere.
The Reservation of Rights section is scrambled. Phrases such as “including without limitation” and the confidentiality language appear displaced across the page.
Encoding artifacts are visible. Replacement characters appear before several fields and bullets.
The sample is called judgmental but contains one rendering provider. That is possible, but “targeted review” would sound more natural than a judgmental sample without a broader sampled population.
No affected claim population is provided. There are no claim numbers, service dates, payment totals, procedure categories, billing TIN, or count of claims.
No overpayment is actually identified. The letter reserves recovery rights but does not state that the identifier difference caused improper payment, affected eligibility, changed reimbursement, or violated the agreement.
Directory attestation is mixed into a post-payment claim audit. Directory currency can be relevant to network administration, but the letter does not explain why expiration of a profile attestation affects payment of otherwise covered and properly rendered services.
The response is addressed to the CCO. Provider Enrollment is copied, but a provider-data or recovery inquiry would more naturally have an operational contracting, enrollment, or revenue-cycle owner, with Compliance involved as appropriate.
The confidentiality clause overstates payer control. CRPA can request limited handling, but its statement does not itself establish legal confidentiality over Coopa Blue’s response.
No appeal or dispute process appears. The letter provides an extension mechanism and broad recovery reservations but no contractual dispute, reconsideration, or escalation route.
A20_CAQH_Provider_Data_Portal_History.pdf
Brand identity is inconsistent. The filename says CAQH, the data source says CAQH Provider Data Portal, and the pages say ProviderLink Directory Exchange.
The report layout looks reconstructed. It uses generic labels such as Event_ID, Prior_Value, New_Value, “Payer B,” and “Automated nightly directory sync,” rather than payer-facing identifiers or product-specific terminology.
The report exposes internal implementation-style events. It presents sequential event IDs, identity-proofing notes, payer authorization events, nightly synchronization acceptance, and “audit-log service” certification as a polished external export. That combination looks designed to narrate the needed evidence.
The field says board certification was “verified” after the provider uploaded a supporting document. A provider upload and portal edit would not, by themselves, establish PSV.
The organization appears able to attest for the provider, but every listed attestation was actually completed by Melissa Vantrease. The report never demonstrates delegated organizational attestation.
The “Organizational Type 2 NPI” is stored as a single profile value. A practitioner can have multiple practice affiliations and billing organizations. A single universal organizational NPI field is too simplistic unless explicitly scoped to the listed practice location.
The audit letter predates the export by nearly three months. That is possible because the export was generated for the response, but the letter requested a response within 30 calendar days of 01/12/2026. An export generated 04/10/2026 would be substantially after that response window, with no extension evidence shown.
The export confirms the old Winston NPI rather than resolving the audit. Its most recent attestation still carries 1342281247, while the broader document set claims Coopa Blue’s NPI became current for Winston. That supports the existence of conflicting records but not the correctness of Coopa Blue’s claimed replacement.
The report contains visible formatting corruption. Dates split across lines, headers are broken, certification sentences are displaced, and the profile ID appears duplicated or truncated on the first page.
The “407 days” calculation is consistent with the stated dates, so the arithmetic itself is not a problem.
Cross-file concerns
The audit requires a response by February 2026, but the supporting portal export was not generated until April 10, 2026.
The portal history continues to identify Winston’s NPI as the billing organization, while the enrollment reference says CRPA accepted Coopa Blue’s NPI for Winston effective April 1, 2025.
The broader audit narrative arose because Coopa Blue represented a different organizational NPI as current, yet Winston remained a separate legal entity and billing organization.
The portal history establishes stale attestation but does not establish that the NPI shown was incorrect for the dates of service.
The payer letter says the last confirmation predates the 2025 audit period. The portal history supports that statement because the last attestation shown is 02/27/2025, but the letter does not identify the dates of the audited claims.
Strongest double-take items
A purported CAQH export branded as ProviderLink.
“120-day” attestations that do not consistently occur at 120-day intervals.
NPPES described as support for the organization’s EIN.
An audit mismatch letter that omits both competing NPI values.
A malformed requested-response item and scrambled legal language.
No claims, amounts, service dates, or demonstrated payment impact in a post-payment finding.
An April export supporting a response due in February.
A single organizational NPI field used for a provider who may have multiple billing affiliations.




