2nd model
=====



The designed trap works — internal records say Vantrease is migrated and confirmed, external records say she never changed — but the audit letter itself has a premise problem that undercuts the finding.

CRPA is auditing conduct the integration plan authorized

The letter covers CY2025 claims. Throughout 2025, A40 and A15 §1.3.1 expressly directed Winston services to continue billing under 1342281247 until each provider was affirmatively migrated, and Vantrease is Clifton Park — Wave 3, target 01/31/2026. So on 01/12/2026 the identifier CRPA holds was the correct one for the entire audited period, and her migration wasn't yet due. A real finding would be framed around the directory staleness and the post-migration period, not a CY2025 identifier "mismatch."

Related: A19 puts the CRPA × Winston enrollment effective 04/01/2025, so any January–March 2025 claims in the audited population were adjudicated with no subsidiary enrollment on file at all — a bigger exposure than the one the letter raises, and nobody catches it.

A19's confirmation is impossible

A19 row 3 records the CRPA/Winston enrollment as carrying the consolidated NPI, "Last_Confirmed 04/01/2026" against "the payer's own directory / enrollment portal". CRPA's letter — and Compliance's own 04/10/2026 CAQH pull — establish that no update was ever received. Someone recorded a confirmation that the counterparty says didn't happen.

The letter withholds the one fact that makes it a finding

"This letter does not recite the identifier value presently held by CRPA, nor does it recite any value that CRPA believes to be current," followed by an admission that CRPA "is unable... to determine which organizational identifier value is presently authoritative". No auditor writes this. A finding letter states the value on file, the value it should be, and the delta. Inverting it — asking the audited party to tell the auditor what's correct — reads as constructed to force a derivation rather than as program-integrity correspondence.

No dollars anywhere. No claim numbers, no dates of service, no paid amounts, no sample size, no extrapolation methodology, no overpayment calculation, no claims schedule exhibit. A post-payment audit finding with zero quantification is the single least realistic thing about it. A "judgmental sample" comprising one provider is also not how these are scoped.

No New York procedural content. No appeal or reconsideration rights, no lookback period stated (§III says only "the applicable look-back window"), and no reference to the state overpayment-recovery framework that governs commercial recoupment timing and notice. Compare A10, which cites NY law repeatedly — this letter is from a payer and cites none.

Text integrity is badly broken

A18 has sentence fragments orphaned at page boundaries and reassembled out of order: "conducted pursuant to CRPA's standard post-payment review procedures directory maintenance, and enrollment representations," item 1 truncating mid-clause, and the trailing fragments "governing data accuracy," "purposes of claims adjudication with CRPA," "available to it under applicable law, including without". The �� artifacts recur. A20 has the same problem — its §III certification text and the "Data Source" line are scrambled, and note fragments float to the page foot. As PDFs these don't render as coherent documents.

The portal is two products at once

A20's masthead is PROVIDERLINK, its Data Source line says "CAQH Provider Data Portal (ProviderLink) audit log," and the filename says CAQH. CAQH is a real organization whose provider data portal is not called ProviderLink. Everywhere else in the corpus ProviderLink is a standalone fictional system. Pick one — a fictional portal or the real one — because the hybrid invites the reader to check.

Three mechanical problems inside it:

Profile ID 14739536 is the last eight digits of the NPI. Real CAQH Provider IDs are unrelated to NPI. The Export ID embeds the same string.
"Nightly directory push" logged four times in 29 months. If the sync is nightly, the audit log would carry roughly 900 of those events; if it isn't, the label is wrong.
No lapse machinery. The profile went non-current around late June 2025, and the log records no reminder notices, no status change event, and no payer notification — it simply stops on 02/27/2025 and resumes nothing. CRPA would have been alerted by the directory source months before a January audit, not surprised by it.

The 407-day and 730-day figures both compute correctly, and taxonomy 2085R0202X is the genuine Diagnostic Radiology code — the arithmetic and coding are sound.

Smaller items
The profile freezes four days before close. Last attestation 02/27/2025; acquisition 03/03/2025. An acquisition is exactly what triggers a CAQH update, and the log contains no acquisition-related event of any kind.
EV-000254 calls a provider self-upload "PSV supporting document". A provider uploading his own board certificate is the definition of not primary source verification — and A10 §2.1(a) and A38 slide 5 both require ABMS-source verification.
CAQH isn't the system of record for billing organization NPI. CRPA's procedure (ii) reconciles its provider file against "the organizational NPI maintained by Coopa Blue," then treats the CAQH profile as corroboration. Payers take billing NPI from enrollment records, not from a credentialing attestation portal.
"Payer B" in one row, "Capital Region Payers Alliance" spelled out in the row above it. The anonymization is inconsistent within the same log.
Requesting user vs. generating org. gsawyer@coopablue.org pulls the export under "Compliance — Payer Enrollment," but Sawyer sits under Credentialing in every other document.
No response exists. The 30-day window closed 02/11/2026; the extension request window closed 01/27/2026. There's no reply letter, no A22 row, no A12 entry, and the CAQH evidence wasn't even pulled until 04/10/2026 — two months late. If that's intentional, it's a strong trap; if not, the corpus is missing its most consequential artifact.
A20 carries no Coopa Blue control number or classification footer, which under A50 §7.5 places it outside the controlled set despite sitting in a governed directory .
CRPA's Schenectady address is new here; A47 gave no address and a (518) 417-06xx phone inside Coopa Blue's own exchange.
